    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d752">

                <pb facs="0865" n="1699" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1699"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 652.119/1308</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in Spam</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Willard</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-08-13T17:00:00-04:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">August 13, 1918, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1423. Referring further your cable of July 31, 11 a.m., Embassy’s 1608,<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d752-f1"/> also your cable of August 2, 6 p.m., Embassy’s
                        1631,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d752-f2"/> duly received.</p>
                <p>We believe any agreement you can make at fixed rates of freight for Spanish
                    tonnage, basis of third proposition, will be acceptable. We, of course, prefer
                    time charter first; second, lump-sum charter, by which they give us the full
                    capacity of the vessel for respective voyages; third, the basis of rates on
                    cargo, weight or measurement varying according to destinations, Italy, French
                    Mediterranean or French Atlantic ports. Any block of tonnage you may secure
                    would be acceptable. The British have asked us to allow the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Serantes, Gloria</hi>, and <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Carasa</hi> to remain on the other side for the ore trade, but we will not
                    consent until we hear from you as to whether this will interfere with your
                    negotiations.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
